        "articleBody": "Last year, we saw Morse Micro introduce the MM8108 Wi-Fi HaLow (802.11ah) SoC with up to 43.33 Mbps throughput, along with an MM8108-RD09 USB dongle reference design, for which we had virtually no details at the time.\r\n\r\nMorse Micro has now released USB-A and USB-C Wi-Fi HaLow adapters based on that reference design. The adapters let PCs and routers connect to 802.11ah networks through USB without a dedicated expansion card and feature an external SMA antenna delivering up to 26 dBm transmit power.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWi-Fi HaLow USB network adapter specifications:\r\n\r\n \tSoC – Morse Micro MM8108 32-bit RISC-V Host Applications Processor (HAP)\r\n\r\n \tStandard – IEEE802.11ah Wi-Fi HaLow\r\n \tFrequency band – 850-950 MHz (Worldwide sub-1 GHz license-exempt range)\r\n \tOperating Modes – Access Point (AP) and Station (STA).\r\n \tChannel Width – 1/2/4/8 MHz\r\n \tModulation – BPSK, QPSK, 16QAM, 64QAM, 256QAM.\r\n \tData rate – Up to 43.3 Mbps using 256-QAM modulation at an 8 MHz bandwidth\r\n \tTransmit power – Up to 26 dBm\r\n\r\n\r\n \tAntenna – SMA connector on the back of the dongle (includes an external Wi-Fi HaLow antenna in the box)\r\n \tUSB – USB-A or USB-C port\r\n \tSecurity\r\n\r\n \tAES\r\n \tSHA-256 / SHA-384 / SHA-512\r\n \tWPA3\r\n \tOWE (Opportunistic Wireless Encryption)\r\n\r\n\r\n \tPower – 5V via USB Type-A or Type-C port\r\n \tDimensions – TBD\r\n \tOperating Temperature –  -40°C to +85°C (silicon/chip — not the enclosed stick)\r\n \tSupported Regions – USA, Canada, Australia, UK, Europe, and Japan\r\n\r\n[caption id=\"attachment_177113\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"] Wi Fi HaLow extends coverage up to 10 times farther than traditional Wi Fi, supporting large sites and multi-building connectivity.[/caption]\r\n\r\nMorse Micro also released the hardware design and manufacturing files for the MM8108-RD09 reference design. The company says the USB adapters support Windows, Linux, and macOS. However, I was unable to find the \"pre-written driver\" for Windows. Linux users need to compile the driver against a patched kernel with S1G (802.11ah) support or use Morse Micro’s OpenWrt fork. The morse_driver and firmware are available on GitHub, along with documentation and resources. More information, including software releases, datasheets, design guides, application notes, and user guides, can be found on the Morse Micro documentation portal.\r\n\r\n[caption id=\"attachment_177129\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"] Internal drawings for the RD09 reference design[/caption]\r\n\r\nThe MM8108 chipset has already been used in modules such as the Morse Micro MM8108-M20 and Quectel FGH200M. This year, the company also released the HaLowLink 2 Wi-Fi HaLow access point based on that same MM8108 HaLow chipset.\r\n\r\nThe Morse Micro Wi-Fi HaLow USB network adapters are currently sold on Mouser for $58.00 in both the USB Type-A and Type-C configurations, with AU variants also available. Additional information is available on the product page.\r\n\r\nVia Hackster.io",
